{
  "step_1": "The decision reason evaluates skewness, specifically whether a lottery is left-skewed—meaning a greater portion of probability is assigned to favorable outcomes (higher monetary payoffs) compared to unfavorable ones. In this case, Lottery A has a 80% chance of winning 4000 Euros and a 20% chance of winning 0 Euros. This means the probability mass is concentrated on the higher outcome (4000 Euros), so A is left-skewed. Lottery B offers 3000 Euros with 100% probability, meaning all outcomes are the same (no variation). Since there is no 'unfavorable' outcome, and all outcomes are favorable and equal, B is not left-skewed. Thus, A is more left-skewed than B. The decision reason would therefore prefer Lottery A. The relevant information for the decision reason is available and clearly derivable from the lotteries' outcomes and probabilities.",
  "step_2": "The individual states: 'Ergebnisse durchgegangen. Für B entschieden, da 100 prozentige chance zu gewinnen.' This translates to: 'Results reviewed. Chose B, because of the 100 percent chance to win.' The individual considers the certainty of the outcome in Lottery B and emphasizes that it offers a guaranteed win. There is no mention of probabilities, outcomes, skewness, or any consideration of the distribution of outcomes. The justification is based solely on certainty, not on skewness or the distribution of favorable versus unfavorable outcomes. Thus, the deliberation process focuses on risk and certainty, not on the skewness of the lotteries.",
  "step_3": "The information relevant to the decision reason (skewness) is not considered by the individual. The individual does not mention the distribution of outcomes or compare the probability mass on favorable versus unfavorable outcomes. Instead, the justification centers on the certainty of winning in Lottery B. The decision reason requires evaluating skewness—specifically, whether a lottery has a greater probability assigned to favorable outcomes (higher payoffs)—which was not part of the individual’s reasoning. Therefore, the decision reason was not used. The individual's reasoning is based on certainty, not on skewness.",
  "final_assessment_score": 0
}

{
  "step_1": "The decision reason evaluates the skewness of the lotteries, specifically whether a lottery is left-skewed (i.e., more probability assigned to favorable outcomes). In this case, favorable outcomes are positive monetary outcomes (e.g., receiving money). For Lottery A: 6000 Euros with 45% probability, 0 Euros with 55% probability. This means the favorable outcome (6000 Euros) has a 45% chance, and the unfavorable outcome (0 Euros) has 55% chance. Thus, the probability of the favorable outcome is less than that of the unfavorable one — the lottery is right-skewed (more weight on the lower outcome). For Lottery B: 3000 Euros with 90% probability, 0 Euros with 10% probability. Here, the favorable outcome has a 90% chance, and the unfavorable outcome has only 10% chance. This indicates a left-skewed distribution (more probability on favorable outcome). Therefore, Lottery B is left-skewed, and Lottery A is right-skewed. The decision reason prefers the more left-skewed lottery, which is Lottery B. Thus, the decision reason is applicable and can be applied to this decision problem.",
  "step_2": "The individual mentions that they 'saw different results' and decided for B because 'the chance for winning is higher'. This indicates that the individual considered the probability of receiving a positive outcome (i.e., winning) and concluded that B offers a higher chance of winning (90% vs. 45%). The justification is based solely on the probability of a positive outcome, not on the skewness of the distribution. There is no mention of comparing the distribution shapes, the magnitude of outcomes, or the asymmetry of probabilities. The individual does not refer to the concept of skewness, left-skewness, or any related statistical property. The reasoning is based on a simple comparison of winning probabilities, not on the skewness of the lotteries.",
  "step_3": "The information relevant to the decision reason — the skewness of the lotteries — is not considered by the individual. The individual does not mention or evaluate the distribution of probabilities across outcomes (e.g., whether favorable outcomes have higher probability than unfavorable ones). Instead, the individual focuses only on the winning probability (90% vs. 45%). The decision reason requires evaluating skewness to determine preference, specifically identifying which lottery has a greater portion of probability on favorable outcomes. Since the individual did not consider skewness or its implications, and instead used a different justification (higher chance of winning), the decision reason was not used. Therefore, the individual did not apply the skewness-based decision reason.",
  "final_assessment_score": 0
}

{
